{"_timestamps":{"start":"2026-05-28T17:25:56.771Z","slide_1":"2026-05-28T17:40:53.435Z","slide_2":"2026-05-28T17:51:59.240Z","slide_2_5":"2026-05-28T18:05:13.115Z","slide_3":"2026-05-28T18:27:13.118Z","slide_4":"2026-05-29T14:30:15.347Z","slide_5":"2026-05-29T16:28:19.027Z","slide_6":"2026-05-29T16:39:38.303Z","slide_7":"2026-05-29T16:48:50.638Z"},"slide_1":"{"part_0":"name of Annie's parents are Joseph and Lena. there home communitythe certificate dated 1955 it records the birth of Annie.","part_1":"the note on the back of the fade photograph says something like Annie and her brother  before school and the picture shows Annie standing with her brother befor they were taken to the residential school.","part_2":"on the school record card Annie's real name was replaced with the assigned name Anne peters the instructor also made a note about her speech saying that her english was  poor and needed a lot of improvement."}","_telemetry":{"slide_1":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":644,"duration_sec":870},"slide_2":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":443,"duration_sec":769},"slide_2_5":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":479,"duration_sec":1374},"slide_3":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":429,"duration_sec":439},"slide_4":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":577,"duration_sec":409},"slide_5":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":281,"duration_sec":258},"slide_6":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":875,"duration_sec":679},"slide_7":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":1211,"duration_sec":562}},"slide_2":"Annie was picking sweet grass and her mother was saying kinship was a circle that cannot be cut she belonged to a large mikmaq family and her grandmother made sure  that she knew where she came from and that kinship tied all of their people together. the mikmaq voern their traditional territory by  self governing people with their own district leadership like local chiefs and the grand council.","slide_2_5":"the shared ancestors at the top of the tree are the great grand parents and they raised Annie and mary in a community built on kinship and seasonal movements.  the status loss for Mary's branch under the indian act Mary likely lost status byt marrying annon indigenous man Annie's branch sixties scoop Annie's descemdemt were likely removed buy child welfare.","slide_3":"{"words":["material","poverty","lack","of","resource","management","Housing","substandard"],"rationale":"material poverty because this means their housing lacks actually innferstructure to be considered a safe area to live in. blames the parents for the effect of systemic economic hardship. Housing substandard because they use external  detached standards with out including the family's cultural or community support system."}","slide_4":"1 a car 2 a clip board and 3her mother crying officials used the phrase best interests to justify removal by assuming nuclear familys were better thank indigenous kinship networks.in 1951 amendment to the Indian act gave the provinces control over indigenous child welfare. As a result about 20,000 indigenous children were removed during the sixties scoop.","slide_5":"{"ans_0":"the name change symbolized the people stealing her identity making her seem like someone who Annie googoo is not.","ans_1":"the kids lost their family and were punished for culture or language and many had long intergenerational trauma."}","slide_6":"Sarah's case and mayas both show colonial control over the indigenous lives Sarah's family was controlled through laws about status and identity , while mayas family was controlled through child welfare and family separation. both systems let the government decide who belong.

Maya asks students to help find the truth because the government records oftend ignore  the pain and experiences of indigenous familey. she believes personal stories can tell her a fuller truth.

these story's show  how colonial government tried to decide what counted as care, belonging, and family instead of indigenous community's deicidng for them selves.","slide_7":"{"part_0":"the government used the Indian act rules to strip first nations status and this ties to mayas grandmothers story by the government both strips the indigenous persons true identity such as their name.  both policies tooks family's and peoples identitie by force with out concent.","part_1":"the Leo's case is like how the provincial child welfare agents took children from reserves because the Leo case was about how the government forced entry on a truck but and the government forced a child to be removed from indigenous reserves both story's include the act of force full actions with out full concent or warning.","part_2":"residential schools caused trama nad broke family's cultural connections. poverty on reserves was later called neglect to justify removing indigenous child from the reserves.\n \nTogether both residential schools  and child welfare worked as one system of colonial control by separatiing children from their families and culture."}"}

{"_timestamps":{"start":"2026-05-29T12:46:25.422Z","slide_1":"2026-05-29T13:03:37.926Z","slide_2":"2026-05-29T13:15:26.856Z","slide_2_5":"2026-05-29T13:33:03.954Z","slide_3":"2026-06-01T04:49:58.593Z","slide_4":"2026-06-01T05:08:10.819Z","slide_5":"2026-06-01T05:17:43.396Z","slide_6":"2026-06-01T15:44:19.308Z","slide_7":"2026-06-01T16:38:55.069Z"},"slide_1":"{"part_0":"Annie's parents were Joseph and Lena Googoo, and her home community was Sipeknekatik First Nation.","part_1":"The note written on the back says, \"Annie & Mary, summer before everything changed\" The photograph shows Annie and a girl named Mary standing together on a wooden porch. This shows that something significant happened after that summer.","part_2":"The assigned name that replaced Annie's name was Anne Peters.  The instructor noted that she was \"Exceptionally quite, refuses to speak, language barrier.\" This suggest she may have struggled because of language differences or the pressure to stop using her own culture."}","_telemetry":{"slide_1":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":836,"duration_sec":1033},"slide_2":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":658,"duration_sec":671},"slide_2_5":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":685,"duration_sec":736},"slide_3":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":611,"duration_sec":1872},"slide_4":{"pasted":true,"keystrokes":472,"duration_sec":177},"slide_5":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":434,"duration_sec":570},"slide_6":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":1788,"duration_sec":1946},"slide_7":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":1927,"duration_sec":1151}},"slide_2":"Annie spent time running along the Shubenacadie River, picking sweetgrass with her grandmother, and learning how to smudge, which shows her string connection to her culture and community. Her mother always said, "Kinship is a circle that cannot be cut," meaning family and community ties are important. The Mi'kmaq governed themselves through their own territory, district leaders, local chiefs, and the Grand Council, showing they have organized systems of leadership long before colonial governments arrived.","slide_2_5":"The shared ancestors at the top of the family tree are Joseph and Lena Googoo, Annie and Mary's parents from Sipeknekatik First Nation. Family unity was disrupted in different ways on both branches. On Mary's Branch, statues loss under the Indian Act affected legal identity and connections to community across generations. One Annie's branch, child welfare apprehension separated her from her family, language and culture after she was taken away and renamed Anne Peters. Even though polices are different but both caused separation and weakened family connection.","slide_3":"{"words":["overcrowding","lack","of","resource","management","immediate","removal"],"rationale":"\"Overcrowding\"focuses only on the living conditions without explaining that Indigenous families faced overcrowding because of government policies, housing and reserve conditions.                      \n\"Lack of resource management\"  This phrase blames the parents for poverty instead of recognizing that many families had limited access to jobs, resources, and support.\n\"Immediate removal\"This shows that a systems immediately separated children from their families instead of trying to help improve conditions at home."}","slide_4":"Three fragments Annie remembers from the day she was taken are a car, her mother crying, and a hand pulling her toward the road. The phrase “best interests” was used with assumptions that non-Indigenous nuclear families were better than Indigenous kinship networks, that poverty meant parents were failing, and that European-style parenting and lifestyles were the proper way to raise children. These beliefs ignored Indigenous cultures and family systems. The 1951 changes to the Indian Act gave provinces authority over Indigenous child welfare,which allowed provincial child welfare agencies to remove children from their communities. As a result, approximately 20,000 Indigenous children were removed during that became known as the Sixties Scoop.","slide_5":"{"ans_0":"This name change ignored Annie's identity, family, connections, language, community, and connection to Sipenknekatik. Changing her name symbolized the erasure of her culture and identity","ans_1":"A child in foster may act this way because they lost their family, home, language, culture, and sense of belonging. Her behavior could reflect trauma and separation rather that something being wrong with her."}","slide_6":"Annie Googoo was a young Mi'kmaw girl from Sipeknekatik First Nation who grew up surrounded by family, culture, and community before being taken from home during the Sixties Scoop. After being removed by child welfare authorities, she was renamed Anne Peters, separated from her family, and pushed to integrate into English culture. Colonial policies created the conditions later used to justify her removal because decades of reserve confinement, poverty, residential schools, and government control caused by overcrowding and limited resources in many Indigenous communities. Instead of addressing these problems, authorities blames families and used child welfare systems to remove children. 

Maya's family story connects to Sarah's because both show different forms of colonial control within the same family line. Sarah's branch experienced status loss through Indian Act, where laws decided who counted legally as Indigenous, while Annie;s branch experienced family separation through child welfare removal. Although the methods were different, both policies weakened family connections, and identity. 

Maya asks students rather than government records to help find the truth because official files often reduce people to categories, labels, and reports while ignoring their humanity and experiences. History told through families, survivors, and communities gives a fuller picture. The survivors stories and problems should not be told by government, because they might not define it correctly, because they don't really understand what truly went threw.","slide_7":"{"part_0":"Sarah's case and Maya's grandmother's experience both show how colonial policies controlled Indigenous identity and disrupted family lines. In Sarah's case, the Indian Act used legal rules to decide who could keep or lose status, affecting identity and community connections across generations. In Annie's case, her grandmother being renamed and having her status marked as \"Inactive\" erased important parts of her identity and connection to her community. Both policies cause separations from their culture and family networks by changing their identity through government systems.","part_1":"In Leo's case it shows how the governments often ignored Mi'kmaw sovereignty and traditional authority systems. This same idea explains why child welfare workers could remove children from reserves without respecting MI'kmaw kinship systems or community leadership. Instead of recognizing Indigenous systems of care and decisions making provincial authorities gave themselves power to decide what was  best for Indigenous families and children","part_2":"Residential schools caused long-term damage to families, language, culture, and community stability, which created trauma that affected later generations. Combined with poverty cause by reserve systems, land loss, and government control, these conditions were later used as a neglect for welfare. Residential schools and child welfare removals worked together as one system because the Residential schools weakened families and communities while the Child welfare used those weakness conditions to justify removing more children. These systems increased government control over Indigenous families."}"}
